$$ where $\mathcal{N}(z,p)=4\pi p^{3}F_{0}/n$ . Note that here $\mu$ is positive and r grows in the direction of wave propagation (i.e., outward from the Sun). Next, consider particles and waves in the shock frame, where the spatial coordinate z grows inward and has z=0 at the shock. Thus, $\partial/\partial r\to-\partial/\partial z$ in the above equations. Close to the shock, particles obey $$
(u _ {1} - v _ {\mathrm{A}}) \frac {\partial \mathcal {N}}{\partial z} = \frac {\partial}{\partial z} \frac {\kappa_ {0}}{\mathcal {P}} \frac {\partial \mathcal {N}}{\partial z}. \tag {A7}
$$ Neglecting spectral diffusion, waves obey $$
(u _ {1} - v _ {\mathrm{A}}) \frac {\partial \mathcal {P}}{\partial z} = \sigma \mathcal {P} = \frac {\pi}{2} \frac {\kappa_ {0}}{v _ {\mathrm{A}} / \omega_ {\mathrm{cp}}} \frac {\partial \mathcal {N}}{\partial z} \equiv a \frac {\partial \mathcal {N}}{\partial z}, \tag {A8}
$$ where $u_{1} = V_{s} - u$ is the upstream bulk speed in the shock frame, so $u_{1} - v_{\mathrm{A}} = V_{s} - V$ is a constant in our model. Thus, $$
\frac {\partial \mathcal {N}}{\partial z} = \frac {u _ {1} - v _ {\mathrm{A}}}{a} \frac {\partial \mathcal {P}}{\partial z}, \tag {A9}
$$ and plugging equation (A9) into equation (A7) gives $$
(u _ {1} - v _ {\mathrm{A}}) \frac {\partial \mathcal {P}}{\partial z} = \frac {\partial}{\partial z} \frac {\kappa_ {0}}{\mathcal {P}} \frac {\partial \mathcal {P}}{\partial z}. \tag {A10}
$$ Integrating once from $-\infty$ to $z$ , we get $$
(u _ {1} - v _ {\mathrm{A}}) \mathcal {P} ^ {2} = \kappa_ {0} \frac {\partial \mathcal {P}}{\partial z}. \tag {A11}
$$ This results in $$
\mathcal {P} = \frac {\mathcal {P} _ {0} z _ {0}}{z _ {0} - z}, \quad \mathcal {N} = \frac {\mathcal {N} _ {0} z _ {0}}{z _ {0} - z}, \tag {A12}
$$ where $P_{0}$ , $z_{0}$ , and $N_{0}$ are functions of momentum related to each other by $$
\mathcal {P} _ {0} = \frac {\kappa_ {0}}{(u _ {1} - v _ {\mathrm{A}}) z _ {0}}, \qquad \mathcal {N} _ {0} = \frac {u _ {1} - v _ {\mathrm{A}}}{a} \mathcal {P} _ {0}. \tag {A13}
$$ At the shock (Bell 1978), $$
\mathcal {N} (0, p) = \mathcal {N} _ {0} (p) = \beta \varepsilon \bigg (\frac {p}{p _ {0}} \bigg) ^ {3 - \beta}, \tag {A14}
$$ where $\beta = 3r_k / (r_k - 1)$ ( $>3$ ) and $\varepsilon$ is the fraction of thermal particles injected into the acceleration process at $p = p_0$ . Thus, $$
z _ {0} (p) = \frac {\kappa_ {0}}{a \mathcal {N} _ {0}} = \frac {\kappa_ {0}}{a \beta \varepsilon} \left(\frac {p}{p _ {0}}\right) ^ {\beta - 3} = h \left(\frac {p}{p _ {0}}\right) ^ {\beta - 3}, \tag {A15}
$$ $$
h = \frac {\kappa_ {0}}{a \beta \varepsilon} = \frac {2}{\pi} \frac {1}{\beta \varepsilon} \frac {v _ {\mathrm{A}}}{\omega_ {\mathrm{cp}}}, \tag {A16}
$$ consistent with the results of Bell (1978). In the case of a distribution of injection momenta, $dN_{inj}/dp_{inj}$ , one can define an effective injection momentum $p_{0}$ by requiring that the shock-processed momentum distribution, $$
\frac {d N}{d p} = \int_ {0} ^ {p} \frac {d N _ {\mathrm{inj}}}{d p _ {\mathrm{inj}}} \frac {1}{(\beta - 3) p _ {\mathrm{inj}}} \left(\frac {p}{p _ {\mathrm{inj}}}\right) ^ {2 - \beta} d p _ {\mathrm{inj}}, \tag {A17}
$$ approaches, at large momenta, the momentum obtained from a monoenergetic injection at $p_{0}$ with the same number of injected particles, i.e., $$
\frac {d N}{d p} \sim \frac {N _ {\mathrm{inj}}}{(\beta - 3) p _ {0}} \left(\frac {p}{p _ {0}}\right) ^ {2 - \beta}, \quad p \gg \langle p _ {\mathrm{inj}} \rangle , \tag {A18}
$$ giving $$
p _ {0} ^ {\beta - 3} = \frac {1}{N _ {\mathrm{inj}}} \int_ {0} ^ {\infty} \frac {d N _ {\mathrm{inj}}}{d p _ {\mathrm{inj}}} p _ {\mathrm{inj}} ^ {\beta - 3} d p _ {\mathrm{inj}}. \tag {A19}
$$ In our model, the injection rate is $$
Q = \frac {N _ {\mathrm{inj}}}{A (r) t _ {\max}} = \varepsilon u _ {1} n, \tag {A20}
$$ so $$
\varepsilon = \frac {N _ {\mathrm{inj}}}{A (r) u _ {1} t _ {\max} n} = \frac {d N _ {\mathrm{inj}} / d \Omega_ {\odot}}{r _ {\odot} ^ {2} u _ {1} t _ {\max} n} \frac {B}{B _ {\odot}} = \frac {d N _ {\mathrm{inj}} / d \Omega_ {\odot}}{r _ {\odot} ^ {2} u _ {1} t _ {\max} n _ {\odot}} \left(\frac {v _ {\mathrm{A}}}{v _ {\mathrm{A} \odot}}\right) ^ {2} \frac {\omega_ {\mathrm{cp} \odot}}{\omega_ {\mathrm{cp}}}, \tag {A21}
$$ giving $$
h = \frac {2}{\pi \beta} \frac {r _ {\odot} ^ {2} u _ {1} t _ {\max} n _ {\odot}}{\mathrm{d} N _ {\mathrm{inj}} / \mathrm{d} \Omega_ {\odot}} \frac {v _ {\mathrm{A} \odot}}{v _ {\mathrm{A}}} \frac {v _ {\mathrm{A} \odot}}{\omega_ {\mathrm{cp} \odot}}. \tag {A22}
$$ Numerically, $$
h = \frac {r _ {k} - 1}{r _ {k}} \frac {V _ {3} t _ {3}}{N _ {3 5}} \frac {v _ {\mathrm{A} \odot}}{v _ {\mathrm{A}}} 5. 0 \times 1 0 ^ {3} \mathrm{km}, \tag {A23}
$$ where $dN_{inj}/d\Omega_{\odot}=N_{35}\times10^{35}$ sr $^{-1}$ , $V_{s}=V_{3}\times10^{3}$ km s $^{-1}$ , and $t_{max}=t_{3}\times10^{3}$ s, and $u_{1}\approx V_{s}$ and $n_{\odot}=4.54\times10^{8}$ cm $^{-3}$ have been assumed. The grid size in our simulations has to be smaller than this, which sets a stringent limit on the maximum simulation time we can reasonably achieve in our model for a large injection strength. In the steady state, the residence time of the accelerated particles in the upstream region is

